# Utility, document, and media FAQs. Imported by guides.py.
FAQ = {}

FAQ['pdf-tools'] = [
("How should I merge, split, or turn scans into a PDF for a real job?",
 "Merge joins files in the order you select them, so name the pieces or select them from first page to last. A contract plus an exhibit should be the contract file first. Split uses a page list such as 1-3, 5 to keep those pages, and Save each page writes one PDF per page when you need to file them separately. Images to PDF places each JPG or PNG on its own page in selection order, which is the right path for phone photos of a receipt stack. There is no fixed page cap. Very large files are limited by the memory of the device because the bytes never go to a server."),
("Which PDF jobs belong together, and which do not?",
 "Merging is for documents that should travel as one file: a proposal and its appendix, or a set of signed pages. Splitting is for the opposite, when a portal wants one page or you must remove a page you did not mean to scan. Image-to-PDF is for pictures, not for editing the words inside an existing PDF. This tool does not reorder pages inside a file with a drag handle, rewrite text, OCR a scan into selectable words, or compress a PDF the way the image compressor shrinks a photo. If you need a smaller PDF, shrink the images first, then build the PDF."),
("Why do some PDFs fail or come out blank?",
 "Password-protected files fail or produce blank pages because the page cannot decrypt them. Remove the password in a reader you are allowed to use, then merge the unlocked file. A truncated download or a file that is only named .pdf will also fail; the page tells you when the file does not look like a PDF. Browser memory is the other limit. Close extra tabs and merge in batches if a few hundred photo pages stall. The result is a normal PDF, not a PDF/A archival file and not a certified signed original. Printing to PDF again can flatten annotations."),
("What are practical file-size and print targets?",
 "A packet you will email should usually stay under a few megabytes. Phone photos dropped straight into a PDF are often several megabytes each. Compress those images to about 1600 pixels on the long side at roughly 75 percent JPEG quality before you convert them to PDF, and a ten-page receipt packet becomes something you can attach. For a packet you will print, 200 to 300 pixels per inch at the printed size is enough for text and ordinary photos. A full-page photo at 300 DPI is about 2550 by 3300 pixels; you do not need a 48-megapixel original in the PDF."),
("How do I keep client documents private and in order?",
 "The work runs in the browser with pdf-lib. Files are not uploaded to GWB and there is no account. That also means there is no cloud trash can: download the result before you close the tab, and keep your own copy of the originals. Do not merge documents you are not allowed to combine, and do not assume a merged file is still the signed original if you changed page order. A checksum or the file size in your download folder is a simple way to confirm the file finished writing."),
("What should I do when the page list does not match the document?",
 "Page numbers in the box are PDF page indexes starting at 1, not the printed number in a footer. A file whose first page is numbered 12 is still page 1 in the box. Open the source and count if the footer and the index disagree. Ranges cannot invent pages past the end of the file. If you need pages from two files, merge first or split each file and then merge the pieces. The tool will not silently drop a password prompt and pretend the merge worked."),
]

FAQ['image-compress'] = [
("What width and quality should I use for web, email, and print?",
 "The width box is a maximum. A 6000-pixel photo set to 1600 pixels wide is scaled down and the height follows, so a 3:2 photo becomes about 1600 by 1067. The default quality is 75 percent, which is the right start for photographs on a website. At 1600 pixels and 75 percent JPEG, a multi-megabyte camera file often lands between about 150 and 400 KB. Use about 1080 pixels for an Instagram post, about 1200 for an email body, and about 400 for a thumbnail. For a hero image you will also print, keep a separate full-size original. This compressor is for screens. A logo you will print belongs in SVG or a PNG at the print size, not a 75 percent JPEG."),
("Why did the file get larger, or why did a PNG barely shrink?",
 "PNG is lossless. Photographs stored as PNG stay large because the format cannot throw away the detail JPEG and WEBP discard. Switch the output to JPG or WEBP for photos. Logos, screenshots, and images with flat color and sharp edges should stay PNG or WEBP lossless so text does not grow halos. If a JPEG comes out larger than the original, the original was already compressed harder than your quality setting. Lower the quality or the width. The status line shows original size, new size, and the percent change so you can compare before you download."),
("What happens to transparency, color, and EXIF?",
 "Choosing JPG fills transparent pixels with white before encoding, because JPEG has no alpha channel. A logo on a transparent background will grow a white box. Use PNG or WEBP if you need the transparency. The browser encoder also drops most camera metadata, including GPS, as a side effect of redrawing the pixels onto a canvas. That is useful when you do not want a location in a file you post, and it is a reason to keep the original if you still need the metadata. Animated GIFs are not preserved as animations; the page draws a bitmap."),
("How do I hit a real target, such as a 200 KB product photo?",
 "Start at 1600 pixels and 75 percent. If you are still over the target, drop quality in 5 to 10 point steps or cut the width to 1200. Product photos on a white background often compress further than foliage or night scenes. Do not upscale. If the original is 800 pixels wide, a 1600 maximum leaves it at 800. Upscaling would invent pixels and usually a larger file. The preview is the file you will download. Judge edges and skin tones there, not by the percentage alone."),
("Is this safe for client photos?",
 "Yes for privacy: the file is decoded and encoded in your browser and is not uploaded. There is no account and no library of your past images on our side. The limit is your device. An image whose pixel count is extreme is refused so the tab does not crash, and a 50 MB cap rejects a file that is unlikely to be a normal photo. Download the result; the object URL disappears when you leave. Keep the camera original somewhere else before you overwrite it."),
("WEBP or JPG for a small-business site?",
 "WEBP is usually smaller than JPEG at the same visual quality and is supported by current browsers. JPEG is the safer attachment when the recipient is a mail program or a marketplace that rejects WEBP. A practical pair is WEBP for the site and JPEG for the marketplace upload. This page writes one format per run. Run it twice if you need both, and do not serve a WEBP file with a .jpg name."),
]

FAQ['image-convert'] = [
("Which format should a business actually pick?",
 "JPG for photographs you will upload to a marketplace, an email, or a tool that rejects newer formats. PNG when you need transparency or hard edges: logos, stamps, screenshots with text. WEBP when the image lives on a modern website and size matters. A logo exported only as a small JPEG will look soft on a retina screen and will show a box wherever the background is not white. Convert that asset to PNG or keep the SVG from the logo maker. Photographs converted to PNG get larger, not clearer."),
("What happens to transparency, and what does quality 0.9 mean?",
 "JPG cannot store transparency. Transparent pixels are painted with the background color you pick, white by default. Set that color to your page color if the logo will sit on a tint, or do not convert that file to JPG. Quality runs from 0.1 to 1 for JPG and WEBP. 0.9 is a light touch and keeps more detail than the compressor's 75 percent default. 1 is still a lossy JPEG, not a mathematical copy. PNG ignores the quality number because it is lossless. Batch conversion uses the same format, background, and quality for every file you selected."),
("Can I convert HEIC, and what fails?",
 "Only if the browser can decode the file. Safari often opens iPhone HEIC photos. Many desktop browsers cannot, and the page will say it could not read the image. On the phone you can set the camera to Most Compatible so new shots are JPEG, or export a JPEG from the Photos app and then convert. AVIF and some CMYK files fail the same way. Rename a .jpg that is actually a PDF and it will fail decode. The extension is not trusted; the browser has to draw the pixels."),
("How do I convert a folder of product shots without mixing them up?",
 "Select the batch, pick JPG or WEBP, and download each result. Keep the original file name in your own folder so the SKU in the name survives. Do not convert the only copy of a transparent PNG to JPG and delete the PNG. Color shifts are possible when a wide-gamut photo is drawn to a standard canvas. For catalog work, compare one image at 100 percent zoom before you convert hundreds. There is no upload queue and no account, so a closed tab is a lost batch if you have not downloaded."),
("What size should the converted file be?",
 "Conversion here does not resize. A 6000-pixel PNG becomes a 6000-pixel JPG, which may still be too large to email. Compress after you convert, or compress first if you already know the target width. A reasonable product image for the web is 1600 to 2000 pixels on the long side. A social avatar can be 512. Print shops often want the original pixels or a PDF, not a twice-converted JPEG. Each conversion that is lossy throws away a little more detail, so convert once from the master."),
("Are the images uploaded?",
 "No. Decoding and encoding happen in the browser. Nothing is stored by GWB and there is no sign-up. You download each file locally. The same privacy boundary is why a failed file cannot be recovered from our side: we never had it."),
]

FAQ['audio'] = [
("MP3 or WAV, and what does this trimmer actually export?",
 "WAV is the uncompressed PCM of the selection, stereo or mono as in the file, at the file's own sample rate, 16-bit. It is the right export when you will edit again. MP3 is encoded in the browser at a fixed 128 kbps with the bundled LAME encoder, which is a small file and plenty for a ringtone or a voice memo. This page does not offer 192 or 320 kbps. If you need a higher-bitrate MP3, export WAV and encode elsewhere, or use the video-to-MP3 tool when the source is a video and you want 192 or 320. A one-minute stereo WAV at 44.1 kHz is about 10 MB. The same minute as 128 kbps MP3 is about 1 MB."),
("How long should a ringtone or alert be, and how do fades work?",
 "Most phones are happy with 20 to 30 seconds. iPhone ringtone files are limited to about 30 seconds, so set the end point at or before 30 if that is the destination. A 1 to 2 second fade-out keeps the clip from clicking at the end. Fade-in can be shorter, often under a second, unless you are softening a loud attack. The fade is a gain ramp on the samples you selected, not a separate effect track. Preview before you export. The selection cannot be shorter than a tenth of a second."),
("Which files open, and what if decode fails?",
 "The browser's audio decoder has to understand the file. MP3, WAV, M4A, AAC, OGG, and FLAC open in current browsers when the codec is supported. A file over about 200 MB is rejected so the tab does not run out of memory. If decode fails, the audio may be corrupt or use a codec the browser cannot play. Convert that file to WAV or MP3 on a machine that can play it, then trim here. The page does not download audio from a URL, a streaming site, or YouTube. It only reads a file you already have."),
("What are the rights and the privacy limits?",
 "Trim audio you own or have a license to use. A purchased song is often licensed for personal listening, not for a remix you publish. This tool will not stop you, and it also will not fetch the song for you. Processing is local. The waveform is drawn from the samples on your device. There is no account and no upload. Export before you close the tab. Memory errors on long files are solved by a shorter selection or by exporting WAV in smaller pieces, not by sending the file to us."),
("How do I make a clean clip for a podcast or a hold message?",
 "Set the start just before the first word and the end just after the last, then add a short fade so you do not cut a breath in half. Voice is intelligible at 128 kbps mono or stereo. Leave the original untrimmed file in your archive. Do not normalize or remove noise here; this page trims and fades only. If the level is too low, fix it in an editor before you export the master. The sample rate of the export matches the source, so a 48 kHz interview stays 48 kHz."),
("Why does the MP3 sound duller than the WAV?",
 "128 kbps throws away some high-frequency detail. On a phone speaker you will rarely hear it. On studio monitors, a music clip will sound tighter in the WAV. Choose WAV when the next step is editing or a master, and MP3 when the file has to be small. Re-encoding an MP3 to MP3 again makes the loss worse. Trim from WAV or from the original when you can."),
]

FAQ['video-to-mp3'] = [
("Which video files work, and what do the bitrates mean?",
 "The browser must be able to decode the video. MP4 with AAC audio and WebM work in most current browsers. Some MOV files and HEVC or HDR phone videos do not, and the page will say it could not decode them. Export MP3 at 128 kbps for speech, 192 kbps as the default balance for music, or 320 kbps when you want the largest MP3 this page makes. WAV is lossless PCM of the decoded audio and is much larger. A five-minute stereo program at 192 kbps is about 7 MB. The same stretch as WAV at 48 kHz is on the order of 55 MB."),
("Can I paste a YouTube or other link?",
 "No. The tool reads a video file that is already on your device. It does not accept a URL, and it is not a downloader. Use it for a screen recording, a voice memo you filmed, a project export, or other video you have the rights to use. Ripping a stream you do not own is not what this page is for and the page cannot do it. If the file will not open, export a standard H.264 MP4 with AAC audio from the app that created it and try that file."),
("Why does a long video fail or stall?",
 "The browser decodes the audio into memory. A long, high-resolution video can exhaust a laptop or a phone even though we only keep the audio. Close other tabs, try a shorter file, or export WAV only for the section you need from a shorter source. The failure is local. There is no server-side encoder to fall back to, which is the privacy tradeoff: your video is not uploaded, so we also cannot finish the job on a bigger machine. A progress or memory message means you should use a smaller file, not refresh and hope."),
("How do I get a usable voice track?",
 "Film or export the video with the microphone you actually want. This page does not split stems, remove music, or boost a quiet voice. 128 kbps is enough for a spoken track you will send by email. 192 kbps is a better default if the track includes music. Keep the video until you have listened to the export. Phase and sync are not an issue because you are taking the whole audio program, not relocking it to picture."),
("What should I check before I publish the audio?",
 "Listen to the start and the end, confirm you exported the right event, and confirm you have the right to publish it. A screen recording can include notification sounds you did not mean to keep; trim those in the audio trimmer after you extract. Do not upload a client's meeting recording to a public host because the extraction was easy. The WAV is the archive copy. The MP3 is the copy you send."),
("Is the video uploaded?",
 "No. Demuxing and encoding run in the browser. There is no account and no retained file. If the tab crashes, the video is still only on your device. Download the audio before you close the page."),
]

FAQ['word-counter'] = [
("How are words, characters, and time counted?",
 "Words are split on whitespace, so a hyphenated word is one word and a number is a word. Characters are shown with spaces and without them. Sentences are split on end punctuation. Paragraphs are blocks separated by blank lines. Reading time uses about 238 words per minute. Speaking time uses about 150 words per minute. A 600-word product description is about 2.5 minutes to read and about 4 minutes to read aloud. Those rates are averages for adult English, not a promise about your reader. The top-keyword list skips very common function words so you can see what you actually repeat."),
("What lengths matter for real business writing?",
 "A search snippet is often cut near 150 to 160 characters, so write the meta description in this box and watch the character count with spaces. Many ad headlines are tighter than 30 characters. A short email people will finish is often under 200 words. A blog post that teaches something real is commonly 800 to 1,500 words, which is a reading time of roughly 4 to 7 minutes at 238 words per minute. The counter does not judge quality. It stops you from discovering the limit after you have pasted into an ad platform."),
("What does keyword repetition tell you, and what does it not?",
 "If one product name is 8 percent of the words, the paragraph may be stuffing rather than explaining. If the name never appears, the page may be vague. The list is a frequency count, not a ranking score and not a guarantee that a search engine will treat the phrase as a topic. Proper names and SKUs count. The same word with different capitalization is grouped in the usual way the counter normalizes. Use it to edit, then read the paragraph aloud."),
("How should I use it with the resume and policy tools?",
 "Resume bullets should usually be one or two lines, roughly 15 to 30 words. Paste a bullet here if you cannot tell whether it is bloated. Policy drafts from the policy generator run long because they have to cover topics; the counter tells you the length so you can see whether a section became a wall of text. Neither tool sends the text to the other. Copy and paste is the handoff, and it stays on the device."),
("Are there limits on length?",
 "Very large pastes are counted locally and can take a moment. The page is built to handle a long document without uploading it. If the tab becomes slow, split the text. There is no account and no stored document. Export PDF, Excel, or CSV if you want the counts and the keyword list in a file, or send a note to your phone. The export is the counts, not a backup of a novel, unless the text is included in that export's inputs."),
("Does it count HTML, code, or languages other than English?",
 "Tags and code are counted as they appear. A page of HTML will show a high word count that includes tags, which is not the reading length of the visible article. Paste the visible text if that is what you care about. Languages without spaces between words will not match an English word count; the character count is still meaningful. Emoji and accents count as characters. The reading-time constant is still the English average."),
]

FAQ['password'] = [
("How random is a password from this page?",
 "Each character is chosen with the browser's crypto.getRandomValues, which is a cryptographic generator, not Math.random. Selection is unbiased across the set you turned on. A 16-character password drawn from about 95 printable characters has on the order of 100 bits of entropy, because each character adds about 6.6 bits. Turn off symbols and you reduce the bits per character. Turn the length down to 8 and you cut the space in half in bit terms, which is a large cut, not a small one. The strength line is an entropy estimate of the settings, not a check against a list of leaked passwords."),
("How long should it be, and should I reuse it?",
 "Use at least 12 to 16 characters for ordinary accounts and longer for email, banking, and the password manager itself. A unique password per account matters more than a clever word. Reuse means one leak opens every site that shares the password. This page does not store the password, does not put it in an export, and does not offer Send to phone, because a notes file or a QR code of a password is a copy you will forget you made. Put it in a password manager. The manager is the memory."),
("What should I do with the result?",
 "Copy it once into the account you are creating and into the password manager. Do not email it to yourself. Do not paste it into a chat with a vendor. If a site rejects symbols, turn symbols off and add length until the strength line is still in the strong range, rather than shortening a complex password to 8 characters. A passphrase of several random words can be appropriate where you must type it often; this generator builds a random string, which is the better shape for a manager-filled login."),
("What does the page refuse to do?",
 "It will not generate a password if every character set is off, because there is nothing to draw from. It does not invent a memorable sentence. It does not check whether a particular site's rules require a number. If a site demands a number, leave digits on. The password is shown so you can copy it. Anyone who can see your screen can see it. Generate it when you are ready to store it, then clear the page if you are on a shared machine."),
("How does this compare with a website's own generator?",
 "A generator on a sign-up form may be fine, and it may also be a script you cannot read. This one is local and does not send the result to GWB. That does not make the password stronger than the math of its length and alphabet. A 6-character password generated perfectly is still short. Prefer this page or your manager's generator over a password built from a pet name and a year."),
("Is anything stored?",
 "No password is written to local storage, to an export file, or to a server. There is no account. Reloading the page gives a new set. If you needed the previous one and did not save it in your manager, it is gone, which is the intended failure mode."),
]

FAQ['business-name'] = [
("How does the generator build a name, and why do results change?",
 "It combines the keyword you type with a style list. Modern, classic, playful, and tech each have their own prefix and suffix pools. Each click draws again, so the list is not a ranked search and it is not exhaustive. A keyword such as bloom bakery can surface compounds and style words around those tokens. The generator does not check whether the name is already a company, a domain, or a trademark. Two people in different cities can be shown the same idea. Treat every line as a starting point you still have to clear."),
("What makes a name usable for a small business?",
 "Say it aloud, spell it to someone over the phone, and see whether the obvious .com or a sensible alternative is something you can register. Short names are easier to put on a sign and a favicon. A playful suffix can undersell a firm that wants to look established, and a classic suffix can make a software product sound like a law office. The style control is there so you can steer that tone. Avoid names that are only a generic word plus Co if you will be impossible to find, and avoid names that describe a regulated profession you are not licensed for."),
("What do I check before I print cards?",
 "Search the USPTO trademark database or your country's equivalent for the exact name and for close spellings in your class of goods. Search your state or national business registry for the legal entity name. Search the domain and the major social handles. A name that is free as an LLC in one state can still infringe a federal trademark. This page does not perform those searches. Budget an hour for clearance before you buy packaging. If you will operate under a name that is not your personal name, file the assumed-name or entity paperwork your state requires."),
("How should I shortlist?",
 "Generate several batches, copy the five you can still remember an hour later, and discard any you hesitate to spell. Check them in the logo maker at a small size. A name that only works in a long script will fail as an app icon. Ask someone outside the business to repeat the name back after hearing it once. Export the shortlist to PDF or a spreadsheet so you can mark which domains you checked. The export is a list you made, not a legal opinion."),
("Can I use a name the tool suggested?",
 "Only after you clear it. The words in the style lists are ordinary English, and your keyword is yours to type, but the combination can still match an existing brand. We do not grant a license to a name, and we do not claim the ideas are unique. If you want a name that is also a registered domain, check the registrar before you fall in love with the spelling. International use needs a check in each country where you will sell."),
("Are my keywords uploaded?",
 "No. Generation runs in the browser. There is no account and no server-side model. PDF, Excel, and CSV exports are local. The keyword is still visible on your screen and in any file you download."),
]

FAQ['email-signature'] = [
("How do I install the signature in Gmail, Outlook, and Apple Mail?",
 "Copy the signature, then paste it into the mail client's signature editor rather than into an individual message. In Gmail that is Settings, See all settings, General, Signature. Create a signature and paste. In Outlook on the web it is Settings, Mail, Compose and reply. Apple Mail uses Settings, Signatures. Paste as rich text if the client asks. A plain-text paste drops the color and the links. Send yourself a test message and read it on a phone, because some apps simplify HTML."),
("What should the signature contain, and what should it leave out?",
 "Name, role, company, one phone number, one email, and one website is enough. A long list of awards, a confidentiality novel, and several social icons makes the signature longer than a short reply. This generator builds a compact HTML block with an accent color and does not upload a photo. If you add a photo in some other tool, host it on a site you control and keep it small, or the image will be blocked and the message will look broken. Do not put a full mailing address in every reply unless you need it for a regulated notice."),
("Why does it look different in some inboxes?",
 "Email HTML is not the web. Clients strip styles, change fonts, and ignore some layout. This generator uses simple inline styles so the important text survives. Dark-mode clients may recolor text. A background color that looked precise on the page can shift. The accent is a guide, not a brand-guidelines proof. If a client must match a brand book exactly, test in that client. Links should be full https URLs. A naked www address is more likely to arrive as dead text."),
("How do I keep it professional when several people share a brand?",
 "Agree the order of lines and the accent color once, then let each person change only their name, title, and phone. Inconsistent signatures look like a forwarded scam. The color palette tool can supply the hex value. Do not include a personal gmail address on a company signature if you want replies in the company system. Quotes and slogans date quickly; leave them off unless they are the actual brand line."),
("Can I export it?",
 "Yes. PDF or Excel keeps a copy of the details for your records. The thing you paste into the mail app is the HTML copy button, not the PDF. The PDF will not install itself as a signature. There is no hosted signature service and no tracking pixel. If you later use a product that hosts signatures, that is a different tool and a different privacy policy."),
("Are my phone number and email uploaded?",
 "No. The preview is built in the browser. There is no account. Copy and paste is a local clipboard action. The details are on your screen and in any file you export, so do not export a personal mobile number into a shared folder by mistake."),
]

FAQ['color-palette'] = [
("Which harmony should I start from?",
 "Analogous colors sit next to each other on the wheel and look calm, which suits a bakery, a clinic, or a long-form site. Complementary colors sit opposite and create a clear accent for buttons and links, at the cost of more tension. Triadic colors are three evenly spaced hues and feel lively if you let one dominate and use the others in small areas. Random is a way to escape a rut, not a brand strategy. Start from the logo color if you have one, then pick the harmony that still looks like you when you desaturate it in your head."),
("How do I keep text readable?",
 "Body text needs a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1 against its background under ordinary WCAG rules, and large text needs at least 3 to 1. A bright accent that looks wonderful as a button can fail as small gray-blue type on white. Use the accent for buttons and lines, and use a near-black or near-white for paragraphs. The swatch label on this page switches between dark and light so the hex stays readable on the chip. That is not a full contrast audit of your site. Check the actual pair you will ship."),
("How do I use the hex values and CSS variables?",
 "Click a swatch to copy the hex. Copy CSS variables when you want the same names in a stylesheet. Put the variables on a single root rule and refer to them so a rebrand is one edit. Do not hard-code five slightly different blues because you sampled them by eye from a screenshot. Record the hex values in the brand sheet export next to the logo file. Colors are not protected as colors, but a distinctive combination used as a brand can still be part of trade dress. The generator does not clear that."),
("What fails in print and in dark mode?",
 "A color that is bright on a screen can be dull or unprintable in CMYK. If you will print packaging, ask the printer for a proof and do not assume the hex is the ink. Very light colors disappear on cheap paper. For dark mode, you usually need a second background and a lighter text color, not the same hex with the lights off. This page generates one palette at a time. Save both if you need a light and a dark pair, and test the button color on both."),
("How many colors should a small brand use?",
 "One background, one text color, one accent, and one optional support color is enough for a site, a signature, and a simple logo. Five equally loud colors make a menu look like a toy. The logo maker only needs a mark color and a background. The email signature needs a single accent. Extra swatches are for illustrations and charts, not for every heading."),
("Are palettes uploaded?",
 "No. The wheel math runs in the browser. There is no account. PDF, Excel, and CSV exports and the phone note are local. A palette is not a logo and not a trademark registration."),
]

FAQ['qr-bio'] = [
("Do these QR codes expire, and what is actually inside them?",
 "The code stores the URL or the text you typed. There is no GWB redirect, no click counter, and no subscription that can turn the code off. The code works for as long as the destination still works and the ink is readable. If you encode a link shortener you do not control, that shortener can expire even though our code did not. For a menu or a card you will print, encode the final https address. Error correction is the library default used by the page, which can recover a modest amount of damage, not a code that has been torn in half."),
("What size should I print, and how do I test it?",
 "For a business card or a table tent, keep the code at least about 2 centimeters (about 0.8 inches) on a side, with a quiet margin of white modules around it. Posters and window stickers should be larger so a phone can read them from the distance people actually stand. Always print one proof and scan it with a phone camera in the lighting you will really have. Dark-on-light works. Light-on-dark and low-contrast brand colors fail. Do not put a logo in the center unless you know the error correction can spare those modules. This generator draws the code, not a branded overlay."),
("How does the link-in-bio file work?",
 "You can download a single bio.html that lists the links you entered. Host that file on any static host, including the same host as your site. The page is yours. It does not phone home to GWB and it does not insert a tracking redirect. Point the QR code at the hosted file if you want one code that can change when you edit the file, or point the QR code at a single URL if you want the code itself to be permanent. Those are different maintenance plans. If the QR points at a file you later delete, the code is fine and the destination is not."),
("What should a shop, a cafe, or a freelancer encode?",
 "A cafe can encode the menu PDF or a page on its own site, not a social profile it might rename. A freelancer can encode a booking page or a vCard-style text block of the details they want public. Do not encode a private invoice link or a password. Anyone who can see the code can open the destination. A Wi-Fi password in a QR code on a window is convenient and also visible to the street. The code is not access control."),
("Why will a code not scan?",
 "The usual causes are too little contrast, too small a print, a missing quiet zone, a glossy lamination glare, or a URL that is so long the modules became tiny. Shorten the URL, increase the print size, and leave white space around the code. Test with a second phone. A code that only scans in the preview and not on paper was printed with scaling that blurred the modules. Print at 100 percent from a PDF or PNG that is not being fit-to-page by the printer dialog."),
("Is the link uploaded?",
 "No. The PNG and the bio file are built in the browser. There is no account, no analytics on the code, and no hosted landing page unless you upload bio.html to a host you choose. That host, not GWB, then sees ordinary web traffic."),
]

FAQ['policy'] = [
("Are these policies legal, and what do I still have to do?",
 "They are plain-English drafts for a small business, not legal advice and not a filing. Laws differ by country, state, and industry. A shop that sells to California residents, takes cards, and runs ads has different duties than a hobby page. Have a lawyer review the draft before you rely on it, especially if you handle health data, children's data, or customers in the EU or UK. The generator fills in the business name, site, email, and location you type and the topics you tick, such as cookies or payments. You must edit anything that does not describe how you actually work."),
("What should a privacy draft cover for a typical small site?",
 "Say what you collect, why, who you share it with, how long you keep it, and how a person can reach you. If you use a card processor, name that role and say you do not store full card numbers if that is true. If you use analytics or ads, say so. If you sell only to businesses in one state and collect nothing but an email, do not claim a global compliance program you do not run. Accuracy matters more than length. A short true policy is better than a long copied one."),
("How should I think about terms and refunds?",
 "Terms of service say what the customer may do, how purchases work, and that the law of a place you name will govern. A refund policy should match what your payment processor and your advertising platforms require, and it should match what you will actually do at the counter. A 30-day window you will not honor is worse than a 7-day window you will. Digital goods, custom work, and perishable food are often treated differently. Write the exception in the draft rather than hoping no one notices."),
("How do I publish and maintain the draft?",
 "Copy the text into a page you control, or download the file and convert it to the format your site uses. Date it. When you add a new tool, a newsletter, or a pixel, update the policy before you turn the feature on. Keep the old date in your own notes so you know what changed. This generator does not host the policy and does not update it when your business changes. The export to PDF or Excel is a snapshot of the draft on the day you made it."),
("What is out of scope?",
 "The drafts are not a GDPR record of processing, a PCI attestation, a HIPAA notice, an employee handbook, or a shareholder agreement. They do not choose your entity type. They do not register you for sales tax. If a sentence feels broader than your business, delete it. If a sentence feels missing, that is a question for counsel, not a reason to invent a legal rule in the text box."),
("Are my business details uploaded?",
 "No. The draft is assembled in the browser. There is no account. PDF and Excel exports are local. You are still about to publish the text on your own site, and that published page is public on purpose."),
]

FAQ['resume'] = [
("What does a strong bullet contain?",
 "A strong bullet starts with an action verb, names the work, and ends with a result a stranger can picture. Handled customer emails is a duty. Answered about 40 customer emails a day and cut the average reply time from a day to under four hours is a bullet. The number can be volume, money, time, or a percent. If you do not have a measured result, use an honest scope: a team of four, a territory of 30 accounts, a class of 25. Do not invent a percentage. Interviewers ask, and a fabricated metric is worse than a plain true sentence."),
("How many bullets, and in what order?",
 "Three to five bullets for a recent role is typical. Older roles can have fewer. Put the result that best matches the job you want first, not the task you did most often. A one-page resume for early career and a two-page resume for a long career are both normal. This tool rewrites one duty at a time so you can paste the best line back into your document. It does not design the page, and it does not invent employers."),
("How do I use numbers I am not sure of?",
 "Use ranges you can defend: about 200 tickets a week, roughly $40,000 in renewals, a queue that fell by around a third. If you cannot defend it, leave the number out. Look at old reports, a CRM export, or a calendar before you guess. The word counter will tell you if a bullet has grown past two lines. A bullet that needs a semicolon and three clauses is usually two bullets."),
("What should stay out of the bullet?",
 "Pronouns, soft skills with no evidence, and the company's marketing slogan. References available upon request is wasted space. Graphics and photos are a liability in many US hiring pipelines and can confuse applicant-tracking systems that read text. Keep the file as text. Do not put a password, a social security number, or a client's confidential metric in a bullet you will upload to a job site."),
("How do the verb styles differ?",
 "The styles change the opening verb so you can hear more than one tone. Pick the one that sounds like the work, then edit the rest of the sentence so the verb agrees with the facts. A louder verb does not make a weak result strong. If none of the lines are true, rewrite the duty more specifically and run it again. The tool is a prompt for clarity, not a biography."),
("Is my work history uploaded?",
 "No. The duty you paste is rewritten in the browser. There is no account and no profile. PDF and Excel exports are local. Clear the box when you are done on a shared computer. The text is only as private as the device and the file you download."),
]

FAQ['logo-maker'] = [
("What do you actually get, and can you use it commercially?",
 "You get a simple text-and-icon mark: your words, one of the original icons drawn for this tool, a layout, a font, and colors. You may use that result for your business. The icons are simple geometric shapes, not a clip-art library we licensed from someone else. You still have to make sure the business name does not infringe a trademark. The generator does not search trademarks and does not file a copyright for you. A logo is not a substitute for a registered mark."),
("What is in the favicon pack, and which sizes matter?",
 "The ZIP includes favicon.ico with 16, 32, and 48 pixel images, PNGs at 16, 32, 180 (Apple touch), 192, and 512, a site.webmanifest, and a short HTML snippet for the head of your site. Browsers use the small sizes in tabs. Phones use the touch icon when someone saves your site to a home screen. 512 is the large icon for an installable web app. Put the files on your site and paste the snippet. A logo that is only an SVG on the about page does not become the tab icon until you add these files."),
("SVG, PNG, or ICO?",
 "SVG stays sharp at any size and is the right master for the web and for a printer who can take vectors. PNG is a bitmap at the size you export, with transparency, and it is what many social and marketplace forms demand. ICO is for the browser tab on older stacks and is inside the favicon pack. A JPEG logo is usually the wrong file because of the white or muddy background and the soft edges. If you will print a sign, give the shop the SVG and ask for a proof. A 512-pixel PNG stretched to a storefront banner will look soft."),
("Why does the font change on another computer?",
 "The preview uses common system fonts so the page does not download a font file. Another machine may substitute a different face. The PNG and the favicon pack bake the pixels, so they look the same everywhere. Use the PNG or SVG for anything you will hand to a printer or upload as an image. Use live text only on your own site if you are comfortable with the fallback. Keep the name short. A long name in a small icon becomes an unreadable stripe."),
("How do I pair it with colors and a real brand?",
 "Pick the mark color from the color palette tool and reuse that hex in the email signature and the site. One icon, one wordmark, one accent. Do not redraw the icon in five colors for five social networks. Test the mark at 16 pixels. If you cannot tell what it is, simplify: fewer words, or the icon alone as the favicon and the full name as the header logo. The transparent background option is what you want on a photo or a colored banner."),
("Is the design uploaded?",
 "No. The preview is drawn in the browser. There is no account and no gallery. Downloads are local, including the PDF brand sheet and the CSV of colors. We do not receive the business name you typed."),
]

FAQ['barcode-label'] = [
("UPC-A, EAN-13, Code 128, or QR: which one goes on the product?",
 "Use UPC-A for a retail product sold in the United States or Canada when you have a GS1-licensed number. UPC-A is 12 digits, and the last digit is a check digit this tool can compute. Use EAN-13 for international retail; it is 13 digits with its own check digit. Use Code 128 for your own SKUs, bin labels, and packing slips. Code 128 carries letters and numbers and does not require GS1, but a retailer will not treat a homemade Code 128 as a UPC. Use QR for a URL or a longer note, not for a POS barcode the scanner at a grocery lane expects to be a UPC."),
("Can I invent a UPC?",
 "You can draw a symbol that is mathematically valid, including the check digit, from any 11 digits you type. Selling that symbol as if it were your licensed UPC is how you collide with another company's product in a retailer's system. License the number from GS1 US or the GS1 office in your country, then type the number they assigned. Internal warehouse labels should use Code 128 and a prefix you control, such as SKU-10042, so they are obviously not retail UPCs. The tool validates the check digit. It does not validate ownership."),
("How do I print Avery-style sheets so they line up?",
 "Download the label sheet PDF and print at 100 percent or Actual size on US Letter. Do not use Fit to page, which scales the sheet and walks the codes off the labels. Avery 5160 is 30 labels, 2.625 by 1 inch. Avery 5163 is 10 labels, 4 by 2 inches, which is easier to scan. Avery 5167 is 80 labels, 1.75 by 0.5 inches, which is tight for anything but a short code. Print a proof on plain paper and hold it against a label sheet in the light before you use the real stock. Leave the human-readable digits on unless you have a reason to hide them."),
("What makes a code fail at the scanner?",
 "Quiet zone too small, bars too thin because the PDF was scaled, lamination glare, a color other than black on white, or a code printed on a curved bottle at a size that is too small. Thermal printers want a high-contrast image and a size the print head can hold. If you use a thermal printer, a single-label PNG or SVG at a known size is often easier than a Letter sheet. Test with the same scanner you will use at the counter, not only with a phone. A phone can read a QR that a laser POS scanner will ignore."),
("How should the text file of codes be prepared?",
 "One code per line. Optionally add a product name and a price separated by vertical bars: SKU-10042 | Blue mug | $14.00. The name and price print on the label. They are not encoded into a UPC, which can only hold the digits. Copies multiplies each line on the sheet. Export CSV or Excel if you want a manifest of what you printed. The PDF is the print file. Keep the text list so you can reprint without retyping."),
("Are the codes uploaded?",
 "No. Barcodes are drawn in the browser with the self-hosted library, and the PDF is built locally. There is no account and no registry of your SKUs. A retail UPC is still a public number once you print it on a product. The privacy of the file is separate from the secrecy of a number you put on a shelf."),
]
